{
 "about": "Workflows: stages in the order they run, what each called agent and each person was given, and the answer. Replayed as conformance_kit.replay_workflow does: `agents` is what each agent answers when called (a list is one answer for each time it is asked, the last repeating); `tools` is what each `<tool>/<action>` hands back; `people` is what a person answers to each question by name (absent: nobody is there to ask, and the run parks). In `expect`: `trace` is the stages that ran, nested ones as `outer/inner`; `given` is each agent called and its inputs, in order; `shown` is each question put to a person and what they were shown; `called` is each tool call. An agent a stage calls is an agent's run, under its own limits, approvals and reactions; here each is answered by the case, so what is compared is the workflow. A case expects only what the specification fixes: where the order things happen in is the runtime's to choose (an `each` with no `at-once-at-most:`), the tree a case runs on says `at-once-at-most: 1`. In `answer`, a field the run did not set is absent, not null; a run that stopped hands back an empty record, and why is in `said`. A trained classifier's answer carries `score` beside its label, a share of one: how sure it was of the label it picked, which `if-unsure-below:` is read against. A person's answer may carry `answered-by` beside the fields the question asks for: who answered, which the stage keeps as `steps.<stage>.answered-by` and does not leave in the answer. On a `decide` that is one name; on a stage that waits for people (`ask`) it is a list, always, in the order they answered, so one name is a list of one.",
